conn.onMessage = async (data) => {
  try {
    const msg = JSON.parse(String(data));
    if ((msg.kind !== "text") || !msg.message || !msg.sessionId)
      throw new Error("bad message shape");

    const sid = msg.sessionId;
    const mode: "safe" | "mature" = msg.mode === "mature" ? "mature" : "safe";
    activeSessions.set(sid, Date.now());

    let reply = "";
    const send = (ev: StreamEvent) => conn.send(JSON.stringify(ev));

    if (mode === "mature") {
      send({ type: "emotion", state: "intimate", intensity: 0.8 }); // placeholder
      for await (const token of streamMatureReply(sid, msg.message)) {
        reply += token;
        send({ type: "token", text: token });
      }
    } else {
      const result = await streamer.streamInteraction(sid, msg.message,
        (ev) => send(ev));
      reply = result.reply;
    }

    // ── Shared memory: BOTH paths land here ──
    const stored = await maybeStoreFact(sid, msg.message);
    await appendTurn(sid, { role: "user", content: msg.message.slice(0, 4000), mode });
    await appendTurn(sid, { role: "assistant", content: reply.slice(0, 4000), mode });

    send({ type: "turn_end", reply, memoriesStored: stored });

  } catch (err: any) {
    conn.send(JSON.stringify({ type: "error", message: err.message } satisfies StreamEvent));
  }
};
Notes on design choices
Why pattern-based fact extraction instead of LLM summarization? It's free, instant, deterministic, and covers the high-value cases (name, location, preferences). If you want smarter distillation later, run a cheap background job that asks the model "what durable facts are in this exchange?" every N turns and merges into facts — the schema already supports it.

Why hardLimits in the system prompt even in mature mode? This is where the line I mentioned lives in code: it's enforced at generation time regardless of which model serves the request. Keep them there even though you control the model — it's the one guardrail that survives config mistakes.

Atomic-ish writes: the .tmp + rename prevents a corrupted session file if the process dies mid-write. Good enough for single-process personal use.

Mode is recorded per-turn, so you can later render or analyze the transcript knowing which register each exchange used.

To customize the persona, edit data/persona.json after first run — no code changes needed.

One last operational reminder: bind the server to 127.0.0.1 (server.listen(WS_PORT, "127.0.0.1", ...)) and keep WS_AUTH_KEY set, since the mature endpoint should never be reachable from other devices on your network by accident.

